---
import { Image, Picture } from "astro:assets";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import landscape from "../assets/landscape.png";
---

<Layout
  title="Images"
  lede="Bunny Optimizer resizes and re-encodes at the edge, from query parameters on the image's own URL."
  badge="Bunny Optimizer"
>
  <div class="card">
    <Image
      id="hero"
      src={landscape}
      widths={[360, 720, 1080]}
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px"
      alt="A generated gradient, resized by Bunny Optimizer"
    />
    <p style="margin:0.9rem 0 0;color:var(--muted);font-size:0.92rem">
      Look at this image's <code>srcset</code>. Each entry is the same file with a different <code
        >width</code
      >, and nothing was resized while the site was built.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Modern formats</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <Picture
      id="formats"
      src={landscape}
      formats={["avif", "webp"]}
      width={720}
      alt="The same image, offered as AVIF and WebP"
    />
  </div>

  <h2>Without the component</h2>
  <p>
    Optimizer works on any URL the pull zone serves, so a
    plain <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> can use it too:
  </p>
  <div class="card">
    <img
      id="plain"
      src="/square.png?width=320&format=webp&quality=80"
      width="320"
      height="320"
      alt="A public image, resized by query parameters"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <pre
      style="margin:1rem 0 0"><code>&lt;img src="/square.png?width=320&amp;format=webp&amp;quality=80" /&gt;</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>Turn it on first</h2>
  <p>
    Bunny Optimizer is a paid pull zone feature, so the adapter never enables it for you. Without it
    the parameters are ignored, and the original image is served.
  </p>
</Layout>
